#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078183 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078423 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9307a5e-2eea-5cea-b5f3-2ce6df53961e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Difference Given Two Consecutive Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "cb23119e-7625-5dad-873f-03049677618c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14385" :question/problem "An arithmetic sequence has $a_{11}=39$ and $a_{12}=34$. What is the common difference of the sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nSince the two terms are consecutive ($a_{12}$ goes right after $a_{11}$ in the sequence), we can find the common difference by subtracting $a_{11}$ from $a_{12}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{12} - a_{11} \\\\\n&= 34 - 39 \\\\\n&=-5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, our result is negative, so we can be fairly confident that it is correct."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078185 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078424 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d15f023-4061-51ad-b307-013bd06d3ec1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Difference Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "a22637f2-14e5-55ff-a41b-1f2a7e0eaa73" :question/math-academy-id "e-14390" :question/problem "Find the common difference of an arithmetic sequence if the $12$th term is $-56$ and the $20$th term is $-112$." :question/worked-solution "The first thing to note is that the terms are *decreasing*, and therefore we expect $d$ to be *negative*.\n\nWe are given the terms\n\n$$\na_{\\color{black}12} = {\\color{red}-56}, \\quad a_{\\color{black}20} = {\\color{blue}-112}\n$$\n\nTo find $d$, we apply the formula with $n=20$ and $m=12$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{n} - a_{m}}{n - m} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{20} - a_{12}}{20 - 12} \\\\\n&= (-112 - (-56))/(20 - 12) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-112 + 56}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-56}{8} \\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $d=-7$.\n\nIndeed, our result is negative, so we can be fairly confident that it is correct."}]}
